   {
    "old": "<figure class=\"figure\" style=\"margin:1em 0\"><a style=\"text-decoration:underline\" href=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/pathways/fa_synthesis.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fa_synthesis-v2-1.svg?v=20261113t\" alt=\"Fatty acid synthesis cycle. Acetyl CoA is carboxylated, using ATP, to malonyl CoA; acetyl and malonyl groups are loaded onto acyl carrier protein (ACP). Step 1, condensation with loss of CO2, gives acetoacetyl ACP (4 carbons). Step 2, reduction by NADPH, gives 3-hydroxybutyryl ACP. Step 3, dehydration, gives crotonyl ACP with a C=C. Step 4, reduction by NADPH, gives butyryl ACP. Butyryl ACP takes another malonyl ACP for the next turn; after 7 turns the chain is palmitate, 16 carbons.\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;width:100%;height:auto;max-width:900px\" loading=\"lazy\" /><img src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fa_synthesis-v2-2.svg?v=20261113t\" alt=\"\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;width:100%;height:auto;max-width:900px\" loading=\"lazy\" /></a>",
